.he "bsh (1)                      10/20/26                     bsh (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
bsh
- the Bourne shell
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
bsh [ -ceiknrstuvx ] [ -c string ] [ file ] [ arg ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Bsh reads commands and runs them.  With a file it reads that file;
with -c it runs the one string it is given and stops; with neither it
reads the standard input.  If the input and the output are terminals
it prompts with PS1, and with PS2 while a command is unfinished, and
it does not exit when interrupted at the prompt.
.sp
A shell whose name begins with a minus is a login shell.  Before
anything else it reads .profile from the current directory.
.sp
Without -c, the first argument, if there is one, is the file to read
and the rest are the positional parameters; $0 is the file.  With -s,
or with no arguments, commands come from the standard input; with -s
the arguments are the positional parameters and $0 is the shell's
name.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-c	string
Run the string and exit.  The arguments after it are $0 and the
positional parameters.

.ti -10
-e	Exit when a command that ran as a child returns a nonzero status.

.ti -10
-i	Interactive: prompt and stay, even when the input is not a
terminal.

.ti -10
-k	Put every word of an assignment command in the environment, not
only the ones before the command name.

.ti -10
-n	Read the commands and check the syntax, but run none of them.

.ti -10
-r	Restricted: refuse to change directory, to run a command whose
name contains a slash, or to write a file with a redirection.

.ti -10
-s	Read commands from the standard input even when arguments are
present; the arguments are then the positional parameters.

.ti -10
-t	Read and run one command and exit.

.ti -10
-u	Treat a substitution of an unset variable as an error.

.ti -10
-v	Write each line to the standard error as it is read.

.ti -10
-x	Write each command to the standard error before it is run,
prefixed with a plus and a space.

.in -10
.sp
The letters are written together in one argument, as in -uc.  -c
takes the argument after the options as its string and may stand
anywhere among the letters.  A second argument beginning with a minus
is not read as options but as the first positional parameter.
.sp
The options may also be set with set; set - turns off -x and -v.  $-
lists the ones that are on.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
WORDS AND QUOTING
.in +5
.br
Words are separated by blanks and tabs, and a line by a newline or a
semicolon.  IFS says what separates words; by default it is space,
tab and newline.
.sp
A backslash protects the character after it, and is removed.  A
backslash at the end of a line continues it.
.sp
A single quote begins a run of characters taken as they stand, up to
the next single quote.
.sp
A double quote takes what is inside it as one word, and suppresses
word splitting and pattern matching on it, but not parameter or
command substitution.  Inside double quotes a backslash protects only
a dollar sign, a backquote, a double quote and a backslash.
.sp
The characters ; & | ^ < > ( ) $ ` ' and the double quote are
special, as are the backslash, the blanks and the newline.  A word
can be built from quoted and unquoted parts joined together, so
a"b"c is one word, abc.
.sp
A word that begins with # begins a comment that runs to the end of
the line; the # must begin a word, so a#b is one word.  Comments are
read from files, from -c strings and from sourced input; at the
interactive prompt itself # is an ordinary character.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PARAMETERS
.in +5
.br
$name, or ${name}, is the value of the variable, empty if it is not
set.  A name is a letter or an underscore followed by letters, digits
and underscores.  The braces are needed when the name is followed by
a letter, a digit or an underscore that is not part of it.

.nf
     ${name-word}   name, or word if it is unset
     ${name=word}   name, assigning word to it if it is unset
     ${name?word}   name, or an error with word if it is unset
     ${name+word}   word if name is set, otherwise nothing
.fi
.sp
Only these forms; the ${name:-word} family is a later shell's and is
a bad substitution here.  A positional parameter may not be assigned
with ${name=word}.
.sp
Substitution after a $ that names no variable is not an error: the $
stands as it was.  The same for a $ at the end of the word.  With -u
an unset variable in ${name-word} style is still not an error, but a
bare $name that is unset is.
.sp
These are always set:

.nf
     $0   the name of the shell or of the file it is reading
     $1..$9  the positional parameters
     $*   the positional parameters
     $@   the same; inside double quotes, still one word each
     $#   how many there are
     $?   the status of the last command
     $!   the process id of the last background command
     $$   the process id of the shell
     $-   the options, as letters
.fi
.sp
Only one digit is taken, so $10 is $1 followed by a zero.  Unquoted,
$* and $@ are one word per parameter.  Inside double quotes "$*" is a
single word with the parameters separated by a space, and "$@" is
still one word per parameter.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
COMMAND SUBSTITUTION
.in +5
.br
A command between backquotes is run and what it writes takes its
place, with trailing newlines removed.  Outside quotes the result is
split into words and matched against patterns; inside double quotes
it is one word.  Backquotes are run inside double quotes too.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PATTERNS
.in +5
.br
After substitution, a word holding one of these is replaced by the
files it matches, sorted:

.nf
     *      any run of characters, including none
     ?      one character
     [...]  one of these; a-z is a range
.fi
.sp
A pattern may appear in any part of a path and is matched one
component at a time.  A leading dot must be matched explicitly.  A
pattern that matches nothing stands as the word that was written.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
REDIRECTION
.in +5
.br
A redirection is a number and an operator, or the operator alone,
which means 1 for output and 0 for input:

.nf
     >file      write file, making it
     >>file     append to file
     <file      read file
     >&digit    copy the output descriptor onto digit
     <&digit    copy the input descriptor onto digit
     >&-        close the output descriptor
     <&-        close the input descriptor
     <<word     here document
.fi
.sp
Any descriptor from 0 to 9 may be named before the operator, so
2>&1 sends the error where the output is going, and 3<file is a third
input.  Redirections apply to the command they are written on, and
when there are several they are taken left to right.  A redirection
with no command before it is made in a child process; it creates or
truncates the file.
.sp
A here document reads the following lines up to one that is the word
alone, and makes them the input.  Parameter, command and backslash
substitution are done on the lines unless the word was quoted, in
which case the lines are taken as they stand.  The lines are kept in
a temporary file under /tmp while the command runs.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
COMMANDS
.in +5
.br
A simple command is a name and its arguments, possibly preceded by
redirections and by assignments.  Assignments before an external
command are in that command's environment only; before a builtin they
are made in the shell.  Without a name the assignments are made in
the shell itself, so

.nf
     name=value
.fi
.sp
sets a variable, and

.nf
     name=value command
.fi
.sp
gives one command the value.  Before the name is looked for, the
command is checked for a reserved word and a builtin.  After that a
name with a slash is run as it is, and one without is looked for
along PATH, which is a colon-separated list; an empty entry means the
current directory.  If the exec fails because the file is not a
valid executable, the shell reads it as a script in a new process.  A
name PATH does not find is reported as not found.
.sp
Commands combine:

.nf
     cmd &          in the background
     cmd1 ; cmd2    in turn; a newline does the same
     cmd1 && cmd2   the second only if the first succeeded
     cmd1 || cmd2   the second only if the first failed
     cmd1 | cmd2    the first's output as the second's input
     cmd1 ^ cmd2    the same pipe
.fi
.sp
and compound commands:

.nf
     (list)                 in a subshell of its own
     { list ; }             in this shell
     if list ; then list ; elif list ; then list ; else list ; fi
     while list ; do list ; done
     until list ; do list ; done
     for name [ in words ] ; do list ; done
     case word in pattern [ | pattern ] ) list ;; ... esac
.fi
.sp
A pipeline binds tighter than && and ||, which bind tighter than ;
and &.  The lists in if, while, until and for end at a newline or
semicolon that is not inside a command, and the reserved words do the
rest.  A for with no in list uses the positional parameters.  break
takes an optional count of loops to leave; continue leaves the one it
is in.
.sp
The status of a command is its exit status, or 128 plus the number
of the signal that killed it.  The status of a pipeline is the status
of its last command.  A command run in the background has status 0.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUILTINS
.in +5
.br
.nf
     . file        read file in this shell; PATH is searched
     :             do nothing, successfully
     break [n]     leave n loops
     cd [dir]      change to dir, or to HOME
     continue      start the next turn of the loop
     eval args     join the arguments and run them
     exec args     run the command in place of the shell; with only
                   redirections, change the shell's own
     exit [n]      exit with status n
     export [name ...]  put the names in the environment of every
                        command run from now on; with no names, list
                        the exported ones
     login [args]  run login in place of the shell
     newgrp [args] run newgrp in place of the shell
     read name ...  read a line from the standard input, split on
                    IFS, and assign the words to the names, with the
                    rest of the line to the last one; status 1 at
                    end of file
     readonly name ...  make the names read only; with no names,
                        list the read only ones
     set [arg ...]  with arguments, set the options and replace the
                    positional parameters; with no arguments, list
                    the variables
     shift         drop the first positional parameter
     trap [cmd] [n ...]  run cmd when signal n is caught; trap n
                    alone resets it; a null cmd ignores it; trap
                    alone prints the traps
     umask [nnn]   set the creation mask for the files the shell
                   creates, or print it
     wait          wait for the background commands
.fi
.sp
There is no echo builtin: /bin/echo is the command.  Signals are
named by number, 0 through 16; 0 is the shell exiting, and the shell
ignores 3 and, when interactive, 15.
.sp
A builtin runs in the shell, so a redirection on one is refused as an
illegal io.  exec is the exception, and on : the redirection is
silently ignored.  The mask from umask applies to the files the shell
opens for redirection; the kernel has no umask of its own, so other
programs are not affected.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
VARIABLES
.in +5
.br
Every variable from the environment is a shell variable and stays in
it.  These mean something to the shell:

.nf
     HOME   where cd with no argument goes
     IFS    what separates words on splitting
     MAIL   a file to check for new mail while prompting
     PATH   where to look for a command; default :/bin:/usr/bin
     PS1    the prompt; default "$ " for a user, "# " for root
     PS2    the prompt for an unfinished command; default "> "
.fi
.sp
The positional parameters, $?, $$ and the rest are set by the shell
and cannot be assigned.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
.nf
     .profile   read by a login shell, from the current directory
     /tmp/sh-*  here documents while their command runs
     /dev/null  the standard input of a background command
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DIAGNOSTICS
.in +5
.br
An error in a command is reported with the command's name and the
reason, and the status is 1.  A syntax error is reported with the
line it was on when the input is not a terminal, and the status is 2.
A command killed by a signal gives the status 128 plus the number of
the signal.  An untrapped fatal signal ends a shell that is not
interactive with status 3; an interactive shell returns to its
prompt.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
sh(1), ccc(1)
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is the Seventh Edition Bourne shell, built with the tree's
compiler and library.  It has no shell functions, no aliases and no
job control.  The times builtin is not here: the kernel has no times
call to answer one.
